The problem states that Katie has 7 pages of math homework and 3 pages of reading homework, with each page containing 9 problems. To determine the total number of problems she needs to complete, we can break it down into two parts: the math problems and the reading problems.

**Step 1: Calculate the total number of math problems**  
Katie has 7 pages of math homework, and each page has 9 problems.  
$$
7 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems/page} = 63 \text{ problems}
$$

**Step 2: Calculate the total number of reading problems**  
Katie has 3 pages of reading homework, and each page also contains 9 problems.  
$$
3 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems/page} = 27 \text{ problems}
$$

**Step 3: Add the math and reading problems together**  
$$
63 \text{ (math problems)} + 27 \text{ (reading problems)} = 90 \text{ problems}
$$

Thus, the total number of problems Katie has to complete is $\boxed{90}$.  
